A||||20||Shiner||Aftermarket Shifter Knobs||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||11-05-1999||02:23 AM||||Does anyone know if the standard universal shifter know (14mm opening) will fit my '87 GT's shifter?  I am looking at the Sparco polished aluminum ball.  I want something nice and big to fit my big hands  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||11-05-1999||10:58 AM||||Aluminum looks good but gets damn hot when it sits in the sun for a couple of hours||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||11-05-1999||01:57 PM||||The diameter of the shifter shaft is 0.625".  The shaft has a metric thread, with a really odd pitch, M16 x 1.5, I think. The stock shaft will not fit into a shifter knob with a 14mm thread.<P>Has anyone seen a generic shifter knob with standard (SAE) 5/8" thread?   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||11-05-1999||03:35 PM||||I am using a MOMO shift knob on mine...I think it's the phantom model..I ported the inside of the shift knob to accomodate my gear shift...works just fine and looks great...||||reg||1
Z||000004||DISenchanted||11-05-1999||05:38 PM||||I did the same thing.  Just take a few cartridge rolls of sand paper and put them on a drill.  Shouldn't take more then 15-20 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||11-05-1999||06:25 PM||||Just had to add, this is my favorite setup, the "sport shifter". I really need to get around to ordering one of these.  <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/shifter.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/shifter.htm</A> <BR>If you guys have never heard of Rodney Dickman, he makes a lot of great stuff for our Fieros.  I have never had any problems with him, and am never afraid of ordering from him.  In the next month or so, he's even coming out with some stickers to replace our worn off shift patterns on the console.  Very slick indeed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eddie||11-05-1999||09:26 PM||||I cut my shifter shaft abuot 2.5" lower threaded it ( sears has right size die) and put a HURST T HANDLE ITS REALY COOL BEST $25.00 UP GRADE EVER TRY IT ILL LOVE IT<BR>	EDDIE||||reg||6
Z||000007||RZURCTN||11-06-1999||12:00 AM||||I have a B&M shift handle somewhere in my garage.  Putting it in my Fiero is something I have been considering for a while.  It just follows me around from truck to car to car....  I wonder how it will work out?  Thanks for bringing this up and reminding me!||||reg||14
Z||000008||Shiner||11-06-1999||02:04 AM||||Well, what shift knobs DO fit the fiero without modification?  I can't imagine myself cutting a big hole in a Momo shift knob; those things are pieces of art!  I would REALLY like something nice and big and round. Does anyone know of a shiny metal ball shifter that DOES fit our cars?  Or maybe somebody that makes 14mm short-shifter kits?||||reg||5
Z||000009||superXmotorsports||11-06-1999||02:08 AM||||I bought a nice Italv Lanti from BC in Canada and waited 2 months for them to get the shipment from Italy.  Guess what?  It wasn't even close to having a large enough inner diameter.  Real nice black and red perforated leather though...||||reg||4
Z||000010||Harry||11-06-1999||09:12 AM||||I cut an old shifter knob down to the threaded insert then installed this into an eight ball. Its nice and big easy to use and I get alot of comments on it.||||reg||14
Z||000011||88 Fiero||11-07-1999||11:39 AM||||I am planning to replace my MOMO shift knob soon... I am in the works of making a Shift Gate for my Fiero..I already ordered a shift gate kit along with the shift gear and it's for the Ferrari kit cars..it looks very nice and I will modify the shift gate to make it work on the factory console of the car...we have a machinist at my work who also got a chance to look of what I am doing and he said it can be done...Once I get this thing finish...I'll have the gate polish and do another MOMO knob...don't like the round type tho'...||||reg||1
Z||000012||jhogans||12-12-1999||02:09 AM||||These guys sell the short shifter.  You can get it with a standard thread pitch to accept custom aftermarket shift knobs from Momo,  Boyds, Hurst, etc.<BR> <A HREF="http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Interior/Interior.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Interior/Interior.html</A> <P>Has anybod used their short shifter?  Has anybody dealt with them?||||reg||1
Z||000013||RICK||12-13-1999||12:09 PM||||I installed a MOMO round shifter knob on my Fiero.  I cut off the orginal stick and rewelded a shorter and small diameter rod in it's place, the leter boot covered up the smaller rod with no problem and it fit the MOMO shifter knob great.  The shifter is now about 2 - 2 1/2 shorter than stock.  I love it.  As far as the aluminum knob goes, mine is aluminum on top, lether on bottom, it gets REAL hot on top on a summer day and REAL cold on a cold day (do not lick the shifter knob)||||reg||1
Z||000014||RICK||12-13-1999||12:11 PM||||BTW take a look at this web site, this is where I go t my shifter knob and pedals: <A HREF="http://www.sportscar-parts.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sportscar-parts.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bruce||12-13-1999||12:31 PM|||| I bought my shortened shifter from Tom- it was $25 and included a shift knob. I cut my stock shift knob down, though, so I could keep the leather cover. the shifter is very well made and arrived within a week. You can e-mail Tom at tmc87@aol.com. His web site is in this forum somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000016||jhogans||12-13-1999||01:20 PM||||How hard is it to install a new shifter?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||12-13-1999||02:11 PM||||If a stick is anything like an auto, it's easy, but time consuming.  The radio/HVAC pod, ECM cover and shift boot all have to come off.  Then there's a plastic lattice work thing (runs the length of the console) that you have to remove.  After that, all you have to do is remove the shift linkage and a few bolts. It really is easy, but it will take a while.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Bruce||12-13-1999||08:41 PM||||For the standard transmission, installing the short shifter takes a maximum of 15 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000019||DJRice||12-14-1999||09:35 AM||||I believe that the short shifter mod does not require replacement of the entire shifter assembly, just removing the pin and replacing the actual stick.  I havent done this one so I dont know for sure but Im thinking thats pretty close to right.  Should be quick and easy.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Alex4mula||12-14-1999||03:54 PM||||I had a beautiful MOMO which I used in the original and the current short shifter. What I did was to use a very coarse (125?) metal grit and reduced the shifter diameter from the top (including the thread) all the way till the MOMO bottom reach. Took me about 45 min. The good thing is that the original thread could still be used with the original knob. Have a vacum cleaner near to suck all the residue. It will get inside everything. Don't ask how I know.....||||reg||7
